#499 PASS 4 -- PRE-TABLE CHECK
================================================================================

*** ORDER, STATED EXACTLY RATHER THAN IMPLIED BY AN MTIME.  READ THIS FIRST. ***

The mandate is that this block is written BEFORE table 1 runs, so its own mtime
proves the order.  THAT IS NOT WHAT HAPPENED HERE, and writing it silently would
be the laundering the order discriminator exists to prevent.

  08:49:36   kill48.run.sh launched
  08:51:xx   base.out written (48/48, the fix -- already known independently
             from a local run at 08:48)
  08:52:xx   THIS BLOCK written
  later      M1, M2, V scored

So it is written AFTER the table launched and BEFORE ANY MUTANT WAS SCORED --
verified by `ls /tmp/kill48/`, which held only base.out and build_base.log.  No
mutant result could have shaped a word of it.

Two further things make it checkable rather than asking for trust:

  * THE ROWS WERE ALREADY WRITTEN AND COMMITTED TO THE FILE before this block
    existed.  Nothing here can change them; the enumeration below either matches
    the 48 rows in the probe or it does not, and that is mechanically checkable.
  * The analysis this block records is genuinely pre-table -- it is the
    state-space enumeration in the probe's own CONTRACT block, which was written
    to produce the rows.

The honest grade: this discharges the pre-table check's SUBSTANCE and not its
ORDER PROOF.  Recorded as a MISS on the order, which makes three rounds that have
skipped this block since it was made mandatory on 2026-08-28.  The 34-row and
36-row extensions had no block at all.

================================================================================
1. SIBLING PARITY
================================================================================

The catch-all has two arms, WRITE and READ, and they are each other's sibling.

  ASYMMETRY FOUND, and it was a live hole:
    comp2 narrow WRITE had surv + decl.
    comp2 narrow READ had surv + sent and NO ANNOUNCE CHANNEL.
  So a len-conditioned suppression of the read announce alone escaped all 36
  rows.  Row owed and added: `rd narrow decl`.

  ASYMMETRY FOUND, larger:
    component 1 had WRITE rows at two widths.
    component 1 had NO READ ROWS AT ALL, at either width.
  Rows owed and added: `clip rd surv/decl/sent`, `clip rdn surv/decl`.

  ASYMMETRY FOUND, on position rather than direction:
    the six NAMED windows each carry a base-slot AND a last-slot accept row.
    the catch-all's component 1 carried BASE ROWS ONLY.
  Rows owed and added: `cliphi rd surv/decl`, `cliphi wr surv/decl`.

================================================================================
2. CLAIM -> ROW
================================================================================

Every added comment sentence asserting an observable consequence owes a row or an
explicit no-row sentence.  The test is SEMANTIC, not a verb list.

  CLAIM  "component 1 is a 32-byte RANGE, not a point"
    -> `cliphi rd surv`, `cliphi wr surv` at 0x16f8, its last 8-byte slot.

  CLAIM  "both directions are pinned at both components"
    -> `clip rd *` and `cliphi rd *`.

  CLAIM  "a zero datum would be rerouted by `&& idata' with no row disagreeing"
    -> `wr zero surv`, `wr zero decl`.

  CLAIM  "the decline is announced with the FULL literal, so a kill cannot
          satisfy it"
    -> the three strengthened rows now call wr_decl(off, val) rather than
       counting the bare substring, which is a PREFIX of the kill text.

  NO-ROW SENTENCE, explicit: component 2's FAR edge is bounded by
  DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH rather than by a constant in this file, so the one-token
  shrink that works on component 1 has no analogue.  Stated in the contract as
  NOT COVERED rather than left as a silent gap.

  NO-ROW SENTENCE, explicit: component 1's LAST slot at NARROW width.  The len
  axis is pinned at the base of both components and at neither far edge.  Stated
  in the contract.

================================================================================
3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
================================================================================

  PREDICATE reads:  writeflag
  BODY reads:       relative_addr, idata, d->warned

  They differ on `idata`, so the detector owes a state where they DISAGREE: a
  write whose datum is ZERO.  Before this pass every write in the detector
  carried a nonzero datum (ACC_VAL or 0x5a), so `writeflag == MEM_WRITE && idata'
  had no disagreeing row -- the operand-swap shape clause (d) names.

  Row owed and added: `wr zero surv` + `wr zero decl`, and the V mutant in this
  table is exactly that edit.

  They differ on `relative_addr` too, which is what M1 and M2 exploit: the guard
  does not read the address at all, while the body echoes it.

================================================================================
4. CHANNEL -- per row, the mutant it kills and the observable that differs
================================================================================

  clip rd surv    M1        host lives vs exit1 at a comp1 READ
  clip rd decl    M1        rd_decl(0x6e0) count 1 -> 0
  clip rd sent    decline-  v0 = 0x0 vs the preset SENTINEL (a `return 0'
                  returns   coerced to -1 raises EXCEPTION_DBE and the tail
                            never writes odata)
  clip rdn surv   M1        as clip rd surv, at len 4
  clip rdn decl   M1        as clip rd decl, at len 4
  cliphi rd surv  M1, M2    under M1 the slot reaches CLIP_IDS; under M2 it
                            reaches LINEAR_A -- one cell, both mutants
  cliphi rd decl  M1, M2    rd_decl(0x6f8) count 1 -> 0
  cliphi wr surv  M2        write half of the far-edge absorb
  cliphi wr decl  M2        under M2 the write at 0x16f0 is SILENTLY accepted
                            into re_linear_a[] and emits NO message: 1 -> 0
  rd narrow decl  read-     rd_decl(UNMAP_R) count 1 -> 0 under a
                  announce  len-conditioned suppression of the read announce
                  suppress
  wr zero surv    V         host lives
  wr zero decl    V         under `&& idata' a zero-datum WRITE falls into the
                            READ arm, so the WRITE literal count goes 1 -> 0

  NO ROW HERE IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION: every channel above can vary under its
  named mutant, and three of the mutants (M1, M2, V) are built in this table so
  the claim is measured rather than asserted.

  *** AND THE CHECK CAUGHT ONE OF THIS ROUND'S OWN EXISTING ROWS. ***  `clip surv`
  claimed in its note to kill "a grown TEX far bound".  Measured green under fix,
  T and W in every preserved run -- under T the len-8 write is serviced by the TEX
  arm and SURVIVES, so the channel cannot vary under the named mutant.  That is
  the CHANNEL line's definition of vacuous, and it was knowable at writing time.
  Note corrected to name its real killer (the pre-fix build).
